/**
 * Execution plan operator icons
 *
 * This file is the single source of truth for the React/Fluent versions of the legacy execution
 * plan PNG icons in `./icons`. Do not hand-edit the traced path data unless correcting a small,
 * understood defect; regenerate a complete icon from its PNG so its layers stay aligned.
 *
 * How the PNGs were converted
 *
 * 1. Start from the original PNG in `./icons` and retain its pixel dimensions. The PNGs remain in
 *    the repository because the classic mxGraph execution plan still consumes them and because
 *    they are the source material for regenerating these vectors.
 * 2. Split the raster into one-bit masks by semantic color. Neutral gray/black pixels form the
 *    `foreground` mask. Blue, purple, red, green, and yellow pixels form separate accent masks.
 *    Classify antialiased edge pixels with the nearest source-palette color so they belong to the
 *    same mask as the solid pixels they soften.
 * 3. Exclude fully transparent pixels and the fixed white matte/knockout pixels. Omitting that
 *    white layer is what lets the same vector render cleanly on both light and dark VS Code themes.
 * 4. Trace each mask independently with Potrace, preserving holes/compound shapes. Use curve
 *    optimization to remove pixel stair-stepping without simplifying enough to change the icon's
 *    silhouette. Export path data only; do not embed the original PNG in an SVG.
 * 5. Keep the source pixel coordinate system as the SVG view box (currently 32, 64, or 128 square)
 *    and preserve the layer order. Add the resulting `d` values to the matching named entry below.
 * 6. Render the neutral layer with `currentColor` and accent layers with VS Code chart color tokens.
 *    The Fluent icon runtime applies `forced-color-adjust: auto`, so forced-color themes can replace
 *    these fills with system colors.
 * 7. Verify the regenerated icon beside its PNG at its native size and at the 30 px execution-plan
 *    size on light, dark, and high-contrast backgrounds. Check that all PNG basenames have an entry
 *    here and that no entry contains an `<image>` element or raster data.
 *
 * Fluent integration
 *
 * `createFluentIcon` normally accepts an array of monochrome path strings. These operators contain
 * semantic accent layers, so `createExecutionPlanOperatorIcon` uses its color-SVG string form. The
 * input is still static, repository-owned path data; no user-provided markup reaches the API. The
 * resulting components support the same sizing, accessibility, `primaryFill`, class name, and ref
 * behavior as icons imported from `@fluentui/react-icons`.
 */

